<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-limits-and-division" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Limits and division
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Prove that
      
      <md>
        \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } } = \frac{ 1 }{ \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } }
      </md>
      
      for every sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of non-zero real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R \setminus \set{ 0 } </m> which converges to a non-zero real number limit
      
      <md>
        \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } \neq 0
      </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m> x = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m>. Since <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> x </m> and <m> x \neq 0 </m>, there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
      
      <md>
        \abs{ x_{ n } - x } \lt \min \of{ \set{ \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } , \frac{ \epsilon \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ 2 } } }
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. We observe that if <m> \abs{ x_{ n } - x } \lt \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } </m>, then
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } \amp = \abs{ x } - \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } \leq \abs{ x - x_{ n } } + \abs{ x_{ n } } - \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } \\
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp \lt \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } + \abs{ x_{ n } } - \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } = \abs{ x_{ n } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      In particular,
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } - \frac{ 1 }{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ x - x_{ n } }{ x_{ n } x } } = \frac{ \abs{ x_{ n } - x } }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } \cdot \abs{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp \leq \frac{ \of{ \frac{ \epsilon \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ 2 } } }{ \of{ \frac{ \abs{ x } }{ 2 } } \abs{ x } } = \frac{ 2 \epsilon \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ 2 \abs{ x }^{ 2 } } = \epsilon
        </mrow>
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Thus
      
      <md>
        \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } } = \frac{ 1 }{ x } = \frac{ 1 }{ \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } }
      </md>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
